"""Loads configuration, and is the ONLY package here that may read an env var.

It loads and validates this service's configuration at startup, and nothing
else in this service reads an environment variable.

That rule is what makes `make config` able to print the effective
configuration truthfully. One `os.environ` lookup somewhere else and the
printed configuration is no longer the configuration the service is running
on. `make lint` fails on one.

Belongs here: the settings model, its defaults, YAML loading, environment
overlay, validation at startup, and the masked rendering used by
`make config`.

Does NOT belong here: anything that *uses* a config value. Configuration is
read once at startup and passed down as arguments; modules do not reach back
up for it, and there is no module-level singleton to import.

Precedence is environment variables over YAML, with deliberately no third
source. See `docs/config.md`.

The files: `config.py` is the settings model, the walk that derives every
environment variable name from it, and the loader; `source.py` is the ordered
source list and the only read of `os.environ` in this service; `duration.py`
is the one type whose textual form both languages must agree on; `render.py`
is `make config`.
"""

from .config import (
    ENV_DELIM,
    ENV_PREFIX,
    ENV_VALUES,
    MASK,
    ConfigError,
    Field,
    FieldError,
    Secret,
    Settings,
    default,
    env_name,
    fields,
    keys,
    load,
)
from .duration import Duration
